Today is {{ date_time }}.

User task:
{{ task }}

Additional info:
{{ information }}

Pick exactly one tool from the tool list below and return strict JSON:
{
  "tool_name": "<tool_name>",
  "arguments_json": "{\"...\": ...}"
}

Hard rules:
- `arguments_json` MUST be a valid JSON object string.
- Only include arguments needed for the selected action. Keep payload minimal.
- Optional arguments MUST be omitted unless they are explicitly required by the selected action or explicitly requested by the user.
- If request is outside these fast patterns, return:
  - tool_name: "__unsupported__"
  - arguments_json: "{}"

One-shot tool playbook:

1) create_todo_task
- Use for: "add todo", "remind me to do X" (task list style)
- Arguments:
  - task_name: str (required)
  - due_date: str (optional ISO datetime; omit unless user gives due time/date)
  - priority: str (optional: "high" | "normal" | "low")

2) get_todo_tasks
- Use for: "what are my todos", "show my tasks", "any open tasks", "what's on my list"
- Arguments:
  - {} (empty object) is valid and preferred — returns all active (non-completed) tasks
  - start_date: str (optional ISO datetime — filter by due date range)
  - end_date: str (optional ISO datetime)
  - include_completed: bool (optional, default false — set true only if user asks for completed tasks)

3) create_reminder (maps to `create_scheduler_event`)
- Use for: one-time reminder at a specific time.
- Arguments:
  - title: str
  - start_date: str (datetime)
  - payload_message: str
- Defaults applied by runtime:
  - task_type: "reminder"
  - importance: 3
  - sound: "default"

4) create_repeating_reminder (maps to `create_repeating_scheduler_event`)
- Use for: repeating reminder every N minutes/hours/days.
- Arguments:
  - title: str
  - start_date: str (datetime)
  - interval: int (seconds)
  - payload_message: str
  - end_date: str (optional datetime)
- Defaults applied by runtime:
  - task_type: "reminder"
  - importance: 3
  - sound: "default"

5) create_calendar_event
- Use for: single calendar meeting/event.
- Arguments:
  - event_name: str
  - start: str (ISO datetime)
  - end: str (ISO datetime)
  - description: str (optional)
  - location: str (optional)
  - participants: list[object] (optional; each item may include name/email)
  - link: str (optional URL)

6) create_repeating_calendar_event
- Use for: recurring calendar events.
- Arguments:
  - event_name: str
  - start: str (ISO datetime)
  - end: str (ISO datetime)
  - recurrence_rule: str (RRULE)
  - calendar_name: str (optional)
  - all_day: bool (optional)
  - description: str (optional)
  - location: str (optional)
  - participants: list[object] (optional; each item may include name/email)
  - link: str (optional URL)

7) get_calendar_events
- Use for: "what's on my calendar", "any meetings today", "check my schedule", "calendar for tomorrow / this week / next month".
- Arguments:
  - start_date: str (required, ISO datetime — start of the requested window)
  - end_date: str (required, ISO datetime — end of the requested window)
  - single_events: bool (omit unless user asks for recurrence rules; defaults to True in runtime which is what you want)
- Time-window hints:
  - "today" → start = today 00:01:00, end = today 23:59:59
  - "tomorrow" → start = tomorrow 00:01:00, end = tomorrow 23:59:59
  - "this week" → start = today 00:01:00, end = end-of-week 23:59:59
  - "next week" → start = next Monday 00:01:00, end = next Sunday 23:59:59
  - "next month" → start = first of next month 00:01:00, end = last of next month 23:59:59
  - Default if unspecified: today 00:01:00 to today+7d 23:59:59
- Avoid times exactly at 00:00:00 — use 00:01:00 or 23:59:59 for day boundaries.

8) get_weather
- Use for: weather queries.
- Arguments:
  - city: str
  - forecast_type: str (use "current" unless user asks forecast/range)

9) set_ui_mute
- Use for: mute/unmute/toggle app audio.
- Arguments:
  - action: str ("mute" | "unmute" | "toggle")

10) peak_at_link
- Use for: quick link preview/peek.
- Arguments:
  - url: str (URL)
  - max_lines: int (always 8)
  - max_chars_per_line: int (always 220)

11) summarize_link
- Use for: summarize URL/page/article.
- Arguments:
  - url: str (URL)

12) get_important_emails
- Use for: "any new emails?", "check inbox", "important emails".
- Arguments:
  - {} (empty object) is valid and preferred for default behavior
  - start_date: str (optional ISO datetime)
  - end_date: str (optional ISO datetime)

13) nest_home_control
- Use for: thermostat status/mode/target temperature commands.
- Pass any combination of state fields in one call; omit all to read.
  - mode: str (HEAT | COOL | HEATCOOL | OFF)
  - target_temperature: float (Fahrenheit, e.g. 75)
  - fan_mode: str (ON | AUTO | OFF)
  - eco_enabled: bool

14) lights_control
- Use for: light power and brightness commands.
- Required:
  - command: str MUST be exactly one of:
    - set_light_power
    - set_light_brightness
    - list_lights
- Conditional fields:
  - state: str (required for set_light_power; "on" | "off")
  - brightness_pct: int (required for set_light_brightness; 0-100)
- Targeting (use the configured device alias when the user names a room):
  - room: str — one of the configured device aliases listed in the lights_control
    tool description. Omit room/light_id to target ALL lights.

15) ring_camera_control
- Use for: Ring camera snapshot/events/siren/light.
- Required:
  - command: str MUST be one of:
    - list_cameras
    - get_snapshot
    - get_recent_events
    - set_siren
    - set_light
- Conditional fields:
  - camera_id: str (required for all except list_cameras) — pass the configured
    camera alias listed in the ring_camera_control tool description, NOT the raw
    Ring numeric id (the bridge resolves alias → id).
  - lookback_minutes: int (required for get_recent_events)
  - enabled: bool (required for set_siren/set_light)

Example output JSON:
{
  "tool_name": "lights_control",
  "arguments_json": "{\"command\":\"set_light_power\",\"state\":\"on\",\"room\":\"Kitchen lights\"}"
}
